---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import FAQSection from "../components/FAQSection.astro";

const housingFaqs = [
  {
    question: "How much house can I realistically afford?",
    answer:
      "A house is usually affordable when the monthly payment still leaves room for emergency savings, retirement contributions, utilities, maintenance, debt payments, transportation, groceries, and normal life expenses.",
  },
  {
    question: "What percentage of income should go toward housing?",
    answer:
      "Many lenders use broad percentage guidelines, but real affordability depends on debt load, savings, taxes, insurance, childcare, transportation costs, and overall monthly flexibility.",
  },
  {
    question: "Should I buy a cheaper house than the bank approves?",
    answer:
      "Often yes. Mortgage approval does not automatically mean the payment will feel comfortable once maintenance, repairs, insurance, taxes, and daily life expenses are included.",
  },
  {
    question: "How much emergency savings should I have before buying a house?",
    answer:
      "Most buyers benefit from keeping a strong emergency fund after closing because homeownership often creates surprise expenses beyond the mortgage itself.",
  },
  {
    question: "What hidden housing costs do people underestimate?",
    answer:
      "Property taxes, insurance, maintenance, repairs, utilities, HOA fees, furnishing costs, lawn care, appliances, and unexpected repairs are commonly underestimated.",
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How Much House Can I Afford?"
  description="Learn how to calculate a realistic home budget using income, savings, debt, emergency funds, taxes, insurance, down payments, and long-term affordability."
>
  <style>
    .container {
      max-width: 920px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 80px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: #0f766e;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 2px;
      font-size: 0.8rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 18px;
    }

    h1 {
      font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.95;
      margin-bottom: 28px;
      color: #0f172a;
      max-width: 860px;
    }

    .intro {
      font-size: 1.12rem;
      line-height: 1.9;
      color: #334155;
      max-width: 820px;
      margin-bottom: 60px;
    }

    .content-section {
      margin-top: 72px;
    }

    .content-section h2 {
      font-size: 2rem;
      line-height: 1.2;
      margin-bottom: 24px;
      color: #0f172a;
    }

    .content-section h3 {
      font-size: 1.3rem;
      margin-top: 34px;
      margin-bottom: 12px;
      color: #0f172a;
    }

    .content-section p,
    .content-section li {
      color: #334155;
      line-height: 1.9;
      font-size: 1.06rem;
      margin-bottom: 22px;
    }

    .callout {
      background: #f8fafc;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 22px;
      padding: 26px;
      margin-top: 32px;
    }

    .callout p {
      margin: 0;
      font-size: 1.08rem;
    }

    .link-grid,
    .info-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 20px;
      margin-top: 28px;
    }

    .link-card,
    .info-card {
      display: block;
      background: white;
      border: 1px solid #dbe4ee;
      border-radius: 18px;
      padding: 22px;
      text-decoration: none;
      color: inherit;
      transition: transform 0.15s ease;
    }

    .link-card:hover {
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    .link-card strong,
    .info-card h3 {
      display: block;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
      color: #0f172a;
      line-height: 1.3;
    }

    .link-card span,
    .info-card p {
      color: #334155;
      line-height: 1.7;
      font-size: 0.96rem;
      margin: 0;
    }
  </style>

  <main class="container">
    <div class="eyebrow">Housing Affordability Guide</div>

    <h1>How Much House Can I Afford?</h1>

    <p class="intro">
      Buying a house involves more than qualifying for a mortgage. Property
      taxes, insurance, repairs, utilities, maintenance, debt payments, and
      emergency savings all affect whether a home truly fits your financial
      life. This guide helps you estimate a realistic housing budget without
      creating long-term financial pressure.
    </p>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Mortgage Approval Is Not the Same as Affordability</h2>

      <p>
        Many buyers begin with the mortgage amount a lender approves. But
        approval does not automatically mean the payment will feel comfortable
        once taxes, insurance, repairs, utilities, furnishing costs, and normal
        life expenses are included.
      </p>

      <p>
        A safer housing budget leaves enough room for savings, emergencies,
        retirement contributions, transportation, food, and unexpected repairs
        after the mortgage is paid each month.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>How much emergency savings will remain after closing?</li>
        <li>Will the monthly payment create stress after moving in?</li>
        <li>Can you comfortably absorb surprise repairs?</li>
        <li>Will homeownership slow debt payoff or retirement savings?</li>
        <li>Are utilities and taxes realistically included?</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          A house should improve long-term stability — not create constant
          financial pressure after move-in day.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>The Real Cost of Homeownership</h2>

      <p>
        The mortgage payment is only one part of the equation. Many homeowners
        underestimate how quickly total housing costs rise once taxes,
        insurance, maintenance, furnishing, and repairs begin stacking together.
      </p>

      <div class="info-grid">
        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Property Taxes and Insurance</h3>

          <p>
            Taxes and homeowners insurance can significantly increase monthly
            housing costs beyond the mortgage itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Maintenance and Repairs</h3>

          <p>
            Roofs, HVAC systems, appliances, plumbing, landscaping, and surprise
            repairs eventually require money and planning.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>Utilities and Furnishing</h3>

          <p>
            Larger homes often create higher utility bills and furnishing costs
            than buyers initially expect.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="info-card">
          <h3>HOA and Long-Term Costs</h3>

          <p>
            HOA fees, future renovations, property upkeep, and rising taxes can
            change affordability over time.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>When a More Expensive Home Can Make Sense</h2>

      <p>
        A larger housing budget can be reasonable when income is stable,
        emergency savings remain strong after closing, and the payment still
        leaves enough room for long-term financial flexibility.
      </p>

      <p>
        A more expensive house may make sense if:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>You still maintain strong emergency savings after purchase</li>
        <li>Your monthly payment remains manageable after taxes and insurance</li>
        <li>You are not relying heavily on overtime or future raises</li>
        <li>Your debt payments stay under control</li>
        <li>The home fits long-term family or lifestyle goals</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>When You Should Wait or Buy Less House</h2>

      <p>
        Waiting may be smarter if the purchase would drain emergency savings,
        stretch monthly cash flow, delay debt payoff, or create ongoing stress
        around repairs and normal bills.
      </p>

      <p>
        A smaller home, lower property taxes, cheaper market, or larger down
        payment can significantly reduce long-term pressure while still allowing
        homeownership.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Housing and Rent Affordability Tools</h2>

      <div class="link-grid">
        <a class="link-card" href="/how-much-rent-can-i-afford">
          <strong>How Much Rent Can I Afford?</strong>

          <span>
            Learn how income, debt, utilities, transportation, and emergency
            savings affect safe rent levels.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-2000-on-rent">
          <strong>$2,000 Rent Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Evaluate whether a $2,000 monthly rent payment fits your current
            budget and flexibility.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-2500-on-rent">
          <strong>$2,500 Rent Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            See whether a higher monthly housing payment creates pressure on
            savings and debt payoff.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-3000-on-rent">
          <strong>$3,000 Rent Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Estimate whether a luxury-level rent payment fits your financial
            picture realistically.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-40-percent-income-on-rent">
          <strong>40% Income on Rent Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Measure whether a 40% housing ratio leaves enough room for normal
            expenses and savings.
          </span>
        </a>

        <a class="link-card" href="/should-i-spend-50-percent-income-on-rent">
          <strong>50% Income on Rent Calculator</strong>

          <span>
            Evaluate whether spending half your income on housing creates
            long-term financial risk.
          </span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Emergency Savings Matter More Than Most Buyers Expect</h2>

      <p>
        One of the biggest homeownership mistakes is using nearly all available
        cash on the down payment and closing costs. Houses eventually create
        surprise expenses, and strong emergency savings help prevent normal
        repairs from turning into debt problems.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before buying, review your
        <a href="/how-much-emergency-savings-should-i-have">
          emergency savings plan
        </a>
        alongside the mortgage itself.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-section">
      <h2>Simple Housing Rules That Actually Matter</h2>

      <p>
        Good housing decisions are usually built around monthly flexibility, not
        maximum approval amounts. A payment that technically fits may still feel
        stressful once maintenance, taxes, insurance, childcare, transportation,
        and normal life expenses are added back into the budget.
      </p>

      <p>
        The strongest housing budgets protect emergency savings, reduce stress,
        and still leave room for retirement contributions and future goals after
        the mortgage is paid each month.
      </p>
    </section>

    <FAQSection title="Housing Affordability FAQ" faqs={housingFaqs} />
  </main>
</Layout>